---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import db from "../../data/score_db.json";

const categories = db.categories;
const tools = db.tools;

// Filter tools by category
const cat1Tools = tools.filter(t => t.categoryNumber === 1);
const cat2Tools = tools.filter(t => t.categoryNumber === 2);
const cat3Tools = tools.filter(t => t.categoryNumber === 3);
const cat4Tools = tools.filter(t => t.categoryNumber === 4);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Architecture Categories"
  activeTab="categories"
  description="Deep architectural taxonomy and evaluation methodology across the 4 SCORE categories."
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="badge badge-primary">FOUR-TIER TAXONOMY</div>
    <h1 class="page-title">The 4 Architecture Categories</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">
      Open-source RISC-V hardware cannot be evaluated as a monolithic collection. SCORE partitions the open hardware ecosystem into <strong>four distinct microarchitectural categories</strong>, each evaluated under a dedicated simulation harness and benchmarking suite.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Quick Category Nav -->
  <div class="category-nav-bar">
    <a href="#category-1" class="cat-pill"><span class="pill-num">01</span> MCU &amp; Scalar Cores</a>
    <a href="#category-2" class="cat-pill"><span class="pill-num">02</span> 64-bit Application Cores</a>
    <a href="#category-3" class="cat-pill"><span class="pill-num">03</span> Vector Coprocessors</a>
    <a href="#category-4" class="cat-pill"><span class="pill-num">04</span> Full SoCs &amp; Clusters</a>
  </div>

  <!-- Category 1 Section -->
  <section id="category-1" class="cat-detail-section">
    <div class="cat-banner accent-yellow">
      <div class="cat-header-top">
        <span class="cat-big-num">01</span>
        <span class="badge badge-accent">LEVEL 1: SCALAR MICROBENCHMARKS</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="cat-heading">Microcontroller &amp; Scalar Embedded Cores</h2>
      <p class="cat-summary">
        Low-power, area-efficient in-order embedded cores optimized for baremetal control, IoT sensing, deeply embedded microcontrollers, and security root-of-trust subsystems. Gate counts range from sub-10 kGE (SERV, PicoRV32) up to 50 kGE (VeeR-EL2, VexRiscv).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cat-body-grid">
      <div class="cat-text-col">
        <h3>Microarchitectural Design Space</h3>
        <p>
          Category 1 cores prioritize area, energy, and deterministic interrupt latency over raw IPC. Across the 12 evaluated cores, architectures diverge dramatically:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Bit-Serial Datapath:</strong> <code>SERV</code> achieves an astonishing ~2.1 kGE by processing one bit per clock cycle over 32 cycles per instruction, making it the world's smallest RISC-V core.</li>
          <li><strong>Multi-Cycle Shared Functional Units:</strong> <code>PicoRV32</code> multiplexes a single adder across instruction decode, ALU operations, and memory address calculation to minimize LUT usage.</li>
          <li><strong>2-Stage Pipelined:</strong> <code>Ibex</code> and <code>SCR1</code> separate instruction fetch from execute/writeback, balancing CoreMark/MHz against branch misprediction penalties.</li>
          <li><strong>3-Stage Harvard:</strong> <code>Hazard3</code> introduces dual-bus fetch/load-store interfaces with dedicated branch prediction and optional custom extensions.</li>
          <li><strong>4-Stage Dual-Issue:</strong> Western Digital's <code>VeeR-EL2</code> incorporates dual-issue superscalar execution for embedded storage controllers, achieving over 3.6 CoreMark/MHz.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout callout-info" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
          <h4>Benchmarking Profile: <code>mcu_verilator</code></h4>
          <p>
            Evaluated using baremetal GCC toolchains without operating system overhead. Standard workloads: <strong>CoreMark</strong> (iterations/sec/MHz) and <strong>Dhrystone 2.1</strong> (DMIPS/MHz).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cat-tools-col">
        <h3>Evaluated Cores ({cat1Tools.length})</h3>
        <div class="mini-tools-list">
          {cat1Tools.map(t => (
            <a href={`/tools/${t.slug}`} class="mini-tool-item">
              <span class="mini-tool-name">{t.name}</span>
              <span class="mini-tool-meta">{t.language} &bull; {t.isa}</span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Category 2 Section -->
  <section id="category-2" class="cat-detail-section">
    <div class="cat-banner accent-teal">
      <div class="cat-header-top">
        <span class="cat-big-num">02</span>
        <span class="badge badge-accent">LEVEL 2: MEMORY &amp; OS COMPLIANCE</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="cat-heading">64-bit Application-Class Cores</h2>
      <p class="cat-summary">
        Linux-capable in-order and out-of-order processors implementing RV64GC with virtual memory management (Sv39/Sv48 MMUs), multi-level split L1 caches, hardware page table walkers, and physical memory protection (PMP).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cat-body-grid">
      <div class="cat-text-col">
        <h3>Microarchitectural Design Space</h3>
        <p>
          Application-class RISC-V cores support multi-user operating systems, virtualized environments, and standard software distributions (Debian, Fedora, Buildroot). Key architectural highlights:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>CVA6 (Ariane):</strong> 6-stage in-order pipeline developed at ETH Z&uuml;rich and maintained by OpenHW Group. Features a 64-bit datapath, Sv39 MMU, branch history table (BHT) + branch target buffer (BTB), and AXI4-64b system bus. Silicon-proven on GlobalFoundries 22FDX and TSMC 16nm.</li>
          <li><strong>Rocket Chip:</strong> UC Berkeley's classic 5-stage generator written in Chisel. Supports highly parameterized cache sizes, branch predictors, and native TileLink interconnect.</li>
          <li><strong>BlackParrot:</strong> Modular multicore processor developed by University of Washington and Bespoke Silicon Group, designed specifically for rapid tapeout in academic MPW shuttles.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout callout-info" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
          <h4>Benchmarking Profile: <code>app_verilator</code></h4>
          <p>
            Evaluates memory subsystem throughput, PMP exception traps, cache line refills, and OS-level workloads: <code>riscv-tests</code> (8 compliance kernels), <code>memcpy</code> throughput sweeps, and Linux boot cycles.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cat-tools-col">
        <h3>Evaluated Cores ({cat2Tools.length})</h3>
        <div class="mini-tools-list">
          {cat2Tools.map(t => (
            <a href={`/tools/${t.slug}`} class="mini-tool-item">
              <span class="mini-tool-name">{t.name}</span>
              <span class="mini-tool-meta">{t.language} &bull; {t.isa}</span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Category 3 Section -->
  <section id="category-3" class="cat-detail-section">
    <div class="cat-banner accent-coral">
      <div class="cat-header-top">
        <span class="cat-big-num">03</span>
        <span class="badge badge-accent">LEVEL 3: DOMAIN VECTOR ACCELERATION</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="cat-heading">Vector &amp; Coprocessor Acceleration Engines</h2>
      <p class="cat-summary">
        Scalable RISC-V Vector 1.0 (RVV) coprocessors, tightly-coupled vector execution units, and systolic matrix multiplication arrays designed to maximize arithmetic density and FLOPS per watt.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cat-body-grid">
      <div class="cat-text-col">
        <h3>Microarchitectural Design Space</h3>
        <p>
          Vector coprocessors decouple instruction fetch from parallel arithmetic execution. Instead of issuing individual scalar instructions, a single vector instruction executes across multi-lane SIMD datapaths:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Ara (RVV 1.0):</strong> Developed at ETH Z&uuml;rich, Ara connects to CVA6 as an autonomous vector coprocessor. It scales from 4 to 16 lanes, supporting vector lengths up to 4096 bits. Each lane contains an integer ALU, vector multiplier, floating-point unit (FPU), and banked Vector Register File (VRF).</li>
          <li><strong>AraXL:</strong> Extended variant featuring clustered multi-banked vector registers to mitigate cross-lane operand routing overheads.</li>
          <li><strong>Spatz:</strong> Ultra-lightweight vector coprocessor paired with the Snitch core, optimized for embedded DSP and ML workloads with minimal silicon area overhead.</li>
          <li><strong>Gemmini:</strong> Systolic array accelerator generator developed at UC Berkeley, supporting parameterized GEMM tiles and quantized integer/floating-point operations.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout callout-warning" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
          <h4>Critical Empirical Finding: Utilization Collapse</h4>
          <p>
            While Ara achieves <strong>98.4% vector efficiency</strong> on dense matrix multiplication (<code>fmatmul</code>), its utilization plummets to <strong>23.0%</strong> on reduction and strided kernels (<code>softmax</code>). The root cause is the scalar core stalling on vector configuration, address stride translation, and scalar-vector operand forwarding.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cat-tools-col">
        <h3>Evaluated Engines ({cat3Tools.length})</h3>
        <div class="mini-tools-list">
          {cat3Tools.map(t => (
            <a href={`/tools/${t.slug}`} class="mini-tool-item">
              <span class="mini-tool-name">{t.name}</span>
              <span class="mini-tool-meta">{t.language} &bull; {t.isa}</span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Category 4 Section -->
  <section id="category-4" class="cat-detail-section">
    <div class="cat-banner accent-green">
      <div class="cat-header-top">
        <span class="cat-big-num">04</span>
        <span class="badge badge-accent">LEVEL 4: FULL SYSTEM SCALING</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="cat-heading">Full SoC Platforms &amp; Multi-Core Clusters</h2>
      <p class="cat-summary">
        Complete System-on-Chip platforms, manycore compute clusters, coherent interconnect fabrics (AXI4, OBI, FlooNoC, P-Mesh), autonomous DMA subsystems, and silicon-tested tapeouts spanning single-chiplet to multi-chiplet topologies.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cat-body-grid">
      <div class="cat-text-col">
        <h3>Microarchitectural Design Space</h3>
        <p>
          Category 4 platforms integrate computing cores with real-world system interfaces: memory controllers, DMA engines, high-speed serial links (PCIe), and on-chip networks:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Snitch Cluster:</strong> High-energy-efficiency cluster pairing pseudo-dual-issue Snitch cores with Stream Semantic Registers (SSR) and Floating-Point Repetition (FREP) to achieve over 80% FPU utilization.</li>
          <li><strong>MemPool:</strong> 256-core manycore architecture sharing a low-latency 1MB L1 Tightly-Coupled Data Memory (TCDM) scratchpad via a multi-stage interconnection network.</li>
          <li><strong>Cheshire:</strong> Minimalist 64-bit Linux SoC architecture featuring CVA6, PCIe Gen3 x2, an autonomous 1D/2D DMA controller, and AXI4 crossbars. Proven on IHP 130nm open-source tapeouts.</li>
          <li><strong>Occamy:</strong> Dual-chiplet 432-core heterogeneous computing platform combining dual-cluster CVA6 management cores, FlooNoC network-on-chip, and HBM2e memory interfaces.</li>
          <li><strong>Picobello / MAGIA:</strong> Innovative synchronization engines replacing software spinlocks with in-network collective hardware reduction and barrier trees.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout callout-warning" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
          <h4>Critical Empirical Finding: The Manycore Scaling Wall</h4>
          <p>
            Multi-core scaling benchmarks across 16, 64, and 256 cores demonstrate that software atomic barriers (AMO) create severe crossbar contention, saturating performance gains past 64 cores. Dedicated in-network synchronization hardware (such as FractalSync in Picobello) restores near-linear speedup up to 256 cores.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cat-tools-col">
        <h3>Evaluated Platforms ({cat4Tools.length})</h3>
        <div class="mini-tools-list">
          {cat4Tools.map(t => (
            <a href={`/tools/${t.slug}`} class="mini-tool-item">
              <span class="mini-tool-name">{t.name}</span>
              <span class="mini-tool-meta">{t.language} &bull; {t.isa}</span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-header {
    background: #ffffff;
    border: var(--noc-border-heavy, 3px solid #1a1a1a);
    box-shadow: var(--noc-shadow-heavy, 6px 6px 0 #1a1a1a);
    padding: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .page-title {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .page-lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: #444444;
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 850px;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .category-nav-bar {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .cat-pill {
    background: #ffffff;
    border: 2px solid #1a1a1a;
    box-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #1a1a1a;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.2rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: #1a1a1a;
    text-decoration: none;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    transition: transform 0.1s ease, box-shadow 0.1s ease;
  }

  .cat-pill:hover {
    transform: translate(-2px, -2px);
    box-shadow: 5px 5px 0 #1a1a1a;
    background: #ffde59;
  }

  .pill-num {
    font-family: var(--noc-font-mono, monospace);
    color: #888888;
  }

  .cat-detail-section {
    background: #ffffff;
    border: var(--noc-border, 2px solid #1a1a1a);
    box-shadow: var(--noc-shadow, 4px 4px 0 #1a1a1a);
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .cat-banner {
    padding: 2rem 2.5rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #1a1a1a;
  }

  .cat-banner.accent-yellow { background: #fffbe6; }
  .cat-banner.accent-teal { background: #e6fcfd; }
  .cat-banner.accent-coral { background: #fff0eb; }
  .cat-banner.accent-green { background: #edfcee; }

  .cat-header-top {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .cat-big-num {
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    font-family: var(--noc-font-mono, monospace);
    color: #1a1a1a;
  }

  .cat-heading {
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .cat-summary {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #333333;
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .cat-body-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr;
    gap: 2.5rem;
    padding: 2.5rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .cat-body-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  .cat-text-col h3, .cat-tools-col h3 {
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #1a1a1a;
    padding-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .cat-text-col p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #333333;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .cat-text-col ul {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem 1.5rem;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .cat-text-col li {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    color: #333333;
  }

  .mini-tools-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    max-height: 480px;
    overflow-y: auto;
    padding-right: 0.5rem;
  }

  .mini-tool-item {
    background: #fbfbfb;
    border: 1px solid #1a1a1a;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.85rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: #1a1a1a;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    transition: background 0.1s ease;
  }

  .mini-tool-item:hover {
    background: #ffde59;
  }

  .mini-tool-name {
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .mini-tool-meta {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: #666666;
  }
</style>
